    explanation: "Beam deflection is how far a loaded beam sags from its straight position, and this beam deflection calculator finds the maximum deflection of a simply supported beam carrying an evenly distributed load. It uses the load per unit length, the span, the material's Young's modulus, and the cross-section's moment of inertia to return the mid-span sag in metres, the point where a uniformly loaded beam bends the most.\n\nThis matters because floors, bridges, shelves, and machine frames must not sag beyond acceptable limits even when they are strong enough not to break. Excessive deflection cracks finishes, causes bouncy floors, and misaligns machinery, so building codes cap it as a fraction of the span. A beam deflection calculator lets you check a design against those limits before choosing a beam size or material.\n\nThe formula works because deflection grows with the fourth power of the span, so length dominates the result, while a stiffer material and a deeper cross-section resist bending through the modulus and the moment of inertia in the denominator. This is why doubling a beam's span increases sag sixteenfold. Structural and mechanical engineers and students use a beam deflection calculator to size beams that stay comfortably within serviceability limits.",
